Subject: DKIM From-header injection attack (non-oversigned)
Message-ID: <INSERT-MESSAGEID-VARIABLE>

This mail demonstrates a From-header injection attack against non-oversigned DKIM.

There are two From: headers. The DKIM signature covers only the bottom one
(INSERT-FROM-VARIABLE) because RFC 6376 specifies that the last occurrence
of each header field is signed. DKIM verification therefore passes.

However, some mail clients display the first From: header, showing
"attacker@evil.com" to the recipient despite the valid DKIM signature.

Countermeasures:

  RFC 5322 requires From to be a singleton header field. RFC 7489 (DMARC)
  Section 6.6.1 states that messages with multiple From fields are typically
  rejected; handling of such messages is outside the scope of DMARC, meaning
  no authentication result is produced and the DMARC policy applies as if
  authentication failed.

  With DKIM oversigning (h=From:From), an injected From header would also
  invalidate the signature. Oversigning is described in RFC 6376 Section 5.4.2.